2. Product Overview
Product name: Reflex

Product type: Live delivery-management prototype

Primary context: Small Kenyan retailers such as electronics shops, pharmacies, and hardware stores currently coordinate deliveries through WhatsApp and phone calls.

Reflex provides a simple centralized delivery-management workflow:

Retailer Staff → Create Delivery → Dispatcher Assigns Rider → Rider Updates Status → Retailer/Dispatcher Sees Live Status

The prototype must demonstrate:

Real-time synchronization of delivery updates

Order/delivery confirmation through QR or barcode scanning

A clear audit trail of important delivery events

The prototype is intended to be simple enough to build and explain within the sprint while being structured well enough to demonstrate professional software-engineering practices.

3. Problem Statement
The current delivery coordination process creates the following problems:

No central delivery record

No clear assignment of deliveries to riders

Poor status visibility

Difficulty knowing which deliveries are still pending

No reliable proof that an order was delivered

Manual communication between retailer staff, dispatchers, and riders

Reflex addresses these problems through a centralized delivery workflow with role-based access, controlled delivery status transitions, real-time updates, QR/barcode confirmation, and delivery-event history.

4. Users and Permissions
Reflex has three required user roles:

Retailer Staff

Dispatcher

Rider

Users must only access functionality appropriate to their role.

4.1 Retailer Staff
Retailer Staff can:

Log in

Create a delivery request

Enter customer name

Enter customer phone

Enter delivery address

Enter item/order description

View delivery status

View delivery details

4.2 Dispatcher
Dispatcher can:

Log in

View unassigned/open deliveries

View available riders

Assign a delivery to a rider

Reassign a delivery when necessary

View delivery status

View delivery history

4.3 Rider
Rider can:

Log in

View assigned deliveries

Open delivery details

Update delivery status

Scan a QR/barcode to confirm the correct order

Confirm delivery

5. Core Delivery Workflow
The primary workflow is:

Retailer Staff → Create Delivery → Dispatcher Assigns Rider → Rider Updates Status → Retailer/Dispatcher Sees Live Status

The required end-to-end workflow is:

Retailer creates a delivery.

Dispatcher views the delivery.

Dispatcher assigns a rider.

Rider sees the assignment.

Rider updates the delivery status through the permitted workflow.

Rider scans the delivery QR code.

The backend validates the delivery and rider.

Delivery is confirmed.

Relevant dashboards update automatically.

The delivery timeline records the event.

6. Functional Requirements
6.1 Authentication
The prototype must implement role-based authentication for:

Retailer Staff

Dispatcher

Rider

Authentication must be enforced by the backend.

Users must only access functionality appropriate to their assigned role.

6.2 Delivery Creation
A Retailer Staff user must be able to create a delivery containing:

Delivery/order ID

Customer name

Customer phone

Address

Item description

Created timestamp

Current status

Assigned rider, if any

6.3 Dispatcher Assignment
The Dispatcher interface must show:

Pending deliveries

Delivery details

Available riders

The Dispatcher must be able to:

Assign a rider

Reassign a delivery when necessary

View delivery status

View delivery history

6.4 Rider Workflow
The Rider must be able to:

View assigned deliveries

Open delivery details

Update delivery status only through permitted transitions

Scan a QR/barcode

Confirm delivery

6.5 Real-Time Synchronization
When a delivery is assigned or its status changes, relevant users must see the update without manually refreshing the page.

6.6 QR/Barcode Confirmation
Each delivery must have a unique identifier that can be represented as a QR code.

The Rider must be able to scan the code using the device camera and use the result to identify or confirm the delivery.

The backend must verify that:

The scanned delivery belongs to the Rider

The delivery is in a valid state

Only after backend validation may confirmation be allowed.

6.7 Delivery History and Audit Trail
Important delivery events must be stored, including:

Delivery created

Rider assigned

Delivery picked up

Delivery moved to out-for-delivery

Delivery delivered

Delivery cancelled/reassigned

Each event must include:

Event type

Delivery ID

User who performed the action

Timestamp

The delivery timeline must provide a clear history of the workflow.

7. State Machine
Delivery statuses are:

PENDING

ASSIGNED

PICKED_UP

OUT_FOR_DELIVERY

DELIVERED

CANCELLED

7.1 Required Transitions
The normal delivery workflow is:

PENDING → ASSIGNED → PICKED_UP → OUT_FOR_DELIVERY → DELIVERED

The specification also requires:

ASSIGNED → CANCELLED

Where appropriate, a delivery may be moved back for reassignment:

ASSIGNED → PENDING

7.2 Transition Rules
Status transitions must be implemented as backend business rules.

The frontend must not be trusted to submit arbitrary statuses.

The backend must validate whether a requested transition is permitted.

Invalid transitions must return an appropriate API error.

